          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you paint polyurethane caulk?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, once it has fully cured, and that is one of its main advantages over silicone. Allow the full cure time on the tube — often several days on a deep bead — before painting, because paint applied over a partially cured polyurethane can wrinkle or stay tacky.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which lasts longer outdoors?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Silicone, in direct sunlight. It is essentially unaffected by ultraviolet light, while polyurethane degrades under sustained UV exposure and can chalk or crack after several years unless it is painted. Painting a polyurethane joint largely removes that weakness, which is why the two are often specified together.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which is better for concrete?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Polyurethane, clearly. It bonds far better to concrete and masonry than silicone does, tolerates the alkalinity, and stands up to foot and vehicle traffic across a joint. Self-levelling polyurethane is the standard product for horizontal expansion joints in driveways, patios and garage floors.",
