    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Polyurethane is the construction sealant and silicone is the weatherproofing one, and that framing predicts almost every sensible answer here. On a typical house you will want both, on different joints, for reasons that have nothing at all to do with which product is "better" in the abstract.
        </p>

        <h2 id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. The Short Answer
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Polyurethane</strong> for masonry, concrete and anything that gets walked on, driven over, or painted. It grips concrete in a way silicone never quite manages, it shrugs off abrasion, and it accepts paint — which matters because most exterior trim schemes are painted.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Silicone</strong> for glass, tile, metal and permanently wet areas, and for any exposed joint in full sun that will not be painted. It is unaffected by ultraviolet light, permanently flexible, and genuinely waterproof.
        </p>

        <p>
          Both are real sealants with movement ratings that make them suitable for joints that expand and contract, which distinguishes both from the acrylic fillers covered in our <a href="/caulk-vs-sealant/">caulk vs sealant guide</a>. The choice between them is about substrate and exposure rather than about quality tiers, and a job that uses each in its proper place beats one that picks a favourite and applies it everywhere.
        </p>

        <!-- Head-to-Head Comparison Table -->
        <div class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl">
          <h2 id="head-to-head" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-8 border-none text-center">Head-to-Head Comparison</h2>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-700">
                  <th class="py-3 px-4 text-[#38BDF8] font-bold text-base">Property</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-4 text-[#38BDF8] font-bold text-base">Polyurethane</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-4 text-[#FF4500] font-bold text-base">Silicone</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-300">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Paintable</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Yes, once cured</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-400">No</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Bond to concrete and masonry</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Excellent</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Fair</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Bond to glass, tile, metal</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Good with primer</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Excellent</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">UV resistance</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-yellow-400">Degrades unless painted</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Essentially unaffected</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Abrasion resistance</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">High — takes traffic</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Low, tears if walked on</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Wet-area performance</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Good</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Best in class</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Cure speed</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-yellow-400">Slow — days on deep beads</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Skins in minutes</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Ease of tooling</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-yellow-400">Sticky and unforgiving</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Clean but fast-setting</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="polyurethane-wins" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Where Polyurethane Wins
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Concrete and masonry adhesion.</strong> This is the decisive advantage. Polyurethane bonds to concrete, block, brick and render far better than silicone, and it tolerates the alkalinity of fresh masonry. Anywhere the joint has cementitious material on at least one side, polyurethane starts ahead.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Horizontal joints that take traffic.</strong> Driveway and patio expansion joints, garage floor control joints and walkway gaps all get walked on and driven over. Silicone tears under that abrasion; polyurethane is tough enough to survive it, and self-levelling versions flow into a horizontal joint and settle flat without tooling.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Paintability.</strong> Because most exterior woodwork and trim is painted, a sealant that disappears under the finish coat is worth a great deal. Polyurethane accepts paint once fully cured, so the joint can be part of the paint scheme rather than a visible line through it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Structural-ish bonding.</strong> Polyurethane's tensile strength is considerably higher than silicone's, which is why construction adhesives and panel bonding products use the same chemistry. On a joint doing a little more than sealing, that strength matters.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Below grade and buried joints.</strong> Where a joint will be backfilled against, polyurethane's toughness and adhesion to masonry make it the standard choice — an area where our <a href="/best-foundation-sealer/">foundation sealer guide</a> covers the wider system it forms part of.
        </p>

        <h2 id="silicone-wins" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Where Silicone Wins
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Ultraviolet stability.</strong> Silicone is effectively immune to sunlight. Polyurethane is not: exposed to years of direct UV it chalks, hardens and eventually cracks, which is why polyurethane joints on sunny elevations are almost always specified to be painted. On an unpainted, sun-facing joint, silicone is simply the more durable material.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Non-porous substrates.</strong> Glass, glazed tile, porcelain, mirror, aluminium and most plastics are silicone's home ground. Polyurethane can be made to bond to several of them, often with a primer, but silicone does it straight from the tube.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Permanent wet.</strong> A shower, a bath surround, a sink or a worktop junction is in contact with water indefinitely, and silicone with a mildewcide is the category built for exactly that.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Colour stability.</strong> Clear silicone stays clear and white silicone stays white. Polyurethane, particularly in white, tends to yellow with age and exposure — invisible under paint, obvious if the bead is exposed.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Speed.</strong> Silicone skins in minutes and is weather-resistant quickly, which matters when the forecast is uncooperative. A deep polyurethane bead can still be soft days later.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Movement Ratings Compared</h3>
        <p>
          Both categories comfortably clear the threshold that separates a sealant from a filler, but they do not sit at the same point on the scale. Quality silicones commonly carry ratings around ±25% to ±50%, and the high end of that range is about as flexible as a residential joint will ever need. Construction polyurethanes typically publish ±25%, sometimes higher, which covers the great majority of expansion and control joints in a house.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where the difference shows is in how each behaves at the limit. Silicone stretches like a rubber band and returns; polyurethane is tougher but slightly less elastic, so it tolerates being pulled and pushed while resisting damage from things dragged across it. That is exactly why silicone suits a joint that moves a great deal and never gets touched, while polyurethane suits one that moves moderately and gets walked on.
        </p>

        <h2 id="working" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Working With Each
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Polyurethane is messy.</strong> It is thick, sticky and clings to everything — tools, gloves, the surrounding surface, and your hands, from which it is very difficult to remove once it starts to cure. Mask both sides of a visible joint, keep mineral spirits and disposable gloves to hand, and tool it in one pass rather than fussing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It cures by drawing moisture from the air</strong>, so cold dry weather slows it markedly and deep beads take days rather than hours. That matters if the joint needs painting, because painting over an uncured polyurethane bead can leave the paint wrinkled or permanently tacky. Check the tube and add a margin.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Silicone is cleaner but less forgiving.</strong> It skins within minutes, so a bead must be tooled immediately and cannot be reworked afterwards. Smears must be removed with solvent before they cure; a damp cloth just spreads them. The common acetoxy type releases acetic acid as it cures — the vinegar smell — and should be kept off natural stone, mirror backing and some metals, where a neutral-cure silicone is the correct choice.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Both want the same joint preparation.</strong> Clean, dry, sound surfaces; backer rod in anything deeper than about a quarter inch; a bead roughly twice as wide as it is deep; and adhesion on two sides only. Get that wrong and the chemistry hardly matters.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cost, Coverage and Colour</h3>
        <p>
          Per tube, the two are closer than their reputations suggest. Polyurethane construction sealants and quality silicones both sit well above the acrylic fillers beside them, and a premium hybrid costs more than either. Across a whole house the difference between chemistries amounts to less than the cost of the ladder you will hire to reach the upper joints.
        </p>
        <p>
          Coverage is worth planning, though, because both are sold by the cartridge and beads consume more than people expect. As a rough guide, a standard cartridge runs roughly 25 to 30 feet of quarter-inch bead, dropping to around 12 feet at half an inch. A house perimeter with windows, doors and penetrations easily consumes a dozen cartridges, and running out mid-elevation means a visible join where two batches meet.
        </p>
        <p>
          On colour, silicone offers the wider range of finished shades because it is never painted, so the manufacturer has to supply the colour you want — clear, white, black, bronze, grey and various stone tones are common. Polyurethane is usually sold in a handful of neutral colours on the assumption that paint will do the rest, and white polyurethane in particular tends to yellow if left bare.
        </p>

        <h2 id="by-job" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Which One, By Job
        </h2>

        <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
          <table class="w-full text-left">
            <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">The Joint</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Driveway expansion joint</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Self-levelling polyurethane</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Concrete bond plus traffic</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shower and bath perimeter</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone with mildewcide</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Permanent wet, non-porous</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Painted exterior trim</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane or hybrid</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Has to take paint</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Glass to frame</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only silicone bonds to glass</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Siding to foundation</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Masonry on one side, paintable</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unpainted sunny joint</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">UV stability</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garage floor control joint</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Self-levelling polyurethane</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vehicle traffic, concrete</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          It is worth saying plainly that <strong>hybrid polymer sealants</strong> increasingly make this comparison academic on residential work. They combine most of polyurethane's adhesion and paintability with much of silicone's UV stability, tool more cleanly than either, and carry long warranties. If you are buying one tube to cover several exterior jobs, a good hybrid is usually the pragmatic answer — our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk reviews</a> cover the products.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Safety and Handling</h3>
        <p>
          Neither product is exotic, and both reward a few sensible precautions. Uncured polyurethane is genuinely difficult to remove from skin — soap and water do very little once it starts to cure — so disposable gloves are worth wearing from the start rather than fetching after the first smear. Keep mineral spirits nearby for tools and overspill, and clean up before the material sets rather than after.
        </p>
        <p>
          Acetoxy silicone releases acetic acid while curing, which is why a freshly siliconed bathroom smells of vinegar for a day. It is an irritant in an enclosed space rather than a hazard, and ordinary ventilation handles it: open a window and leave the room to cure. Both chemistries want the label read for the specifics, particularly around confined spaces and cure times before the joint is exposed to water.
        </p>
        <p>
          One practical habit that improves both: bring cartridges up to room temperature before use. Cold sealant is stiff, extrudes unevenly, and tools badly, and a cartridge that has spent the night in a van in January will fight you the whole way along the joint. Twenty minutes indoors makes a noticeable difference to the finished bead.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Polyurethane Unpainted in Full Sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                UV degrades it over a few years — chalking, hardening, then cracking. Either paint the bead as intended, or use silicone or a hybrid on exposed joints that will stay bare.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Silicone in a Concrete Expansion Joint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It bonds less well to concrete and tears under foot and vehicle traffic. Horizontal joints in slabs want self-levelling polyurethane, which flows flat and survives being driven over.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Polyurethane Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It cures slowly from the outside in, and paint over a soft bead wrinkles or stays tacky. On a deep joint, allow days rather than hours before the brush comes anywhere near it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Acetoxy Silicone on Stone or Metal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The acetic acid released during cure can etch natural stone and corrode some metals and mirror backing. On those substrates, buy a neutral-cure silicone specifically.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to Recaulk Over Either One</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nothing bonds reliably to cured silicone, and polyurethane over it fails quickly. Cut the old bead out and clean the joint back to bare material before applying anything new.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          A final practical note on sequencing when a job uses both. Do the polyurethane work first, let it cure and take its paint, and run the silicone beads afterwards. Silicone contamination is the enemy of everything else on a building — even a smear transferred on a glove can stop paint or polyurethane bonding where it lands — so keeping it to the end of the job avoids a category of problem that is very hard to diagnose afterwards.
        </p>

        <!-- Verdict -->
        <div id="verdict" class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
            ★ Our Verdict
          </div>
          <h2 id="who-wins" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">Polyurethane vs Silicone: Who Wins?</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#38BDF8]">Choose polyurethane if:</strong> the joint involves concrete or masonry, gets walked or driven on, or has to be painted. Driveway and garage floor joints, siding-to-foundation lines and painted exterior trim all belong to it, and its abrasion resistance and adhesion are not close.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Choose silicone if:</strong> the joint involves glass, tile or metal, stays permanently wet, or sits exposed in sunlight without paint. Bathrooms, kitchens, glazing and unpainted weather joints are its territory, and its UV stability is unmatched.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> These are complementary rather than competing. A typical house wants polyurethane in the slab joints and painted trim, and silicone in the bathroom and against the glass. If you would rather buy one tube and stop thinking about it, a quality hybrid polymer covers most of both jobs — at a price that still looks cheap next to cutting out a failed bead and starting again.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Polyurethane bonds exceptionally to concrete and masonry, takes paint, and resists abrasion — the construction sealant. Silicone bonds better to glass, tile and metal, shrugs off UV, and is the right choice in wet areas, but cannot be painted.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint polyurethane caulk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, once fully cured — one of its main advantages. Allow the full cure time, often several days on a deep bead, because paint over partially cured polyurethane can wrinkle or stay tacky.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which lasts longer outdoors?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Silicone in direct sun — it is essentially unaffected by UV, while polyurethane chalks and cracks after several years unless painted. Painting a polyurethane joint largely removes that weakness, which is why the two are often specified together.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Polyurethane, clearly. It bonds far better to concrete and masonry, tolerates alkalinity, and survives traffic across the joint. Self-levelling polyurethane is standard for horizontal expansion joints in driveways, patios and garage floors.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for bathrooms?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Silicone, with a mildewcide. Wet areas are non-porous surfaces in permanent contact with water — exactly what silicone is formulated for and polyurethane is not.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is polyurethane harder to work with?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes — thicker, stickier, clings to tools and gloves, and cures slowly by drawing moisture from the air, so deep beads take days. Silicone skins in minutes and tools more cleanly, but is unforgiving once setting and needs solvent for cleanup.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply one over the other?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Polyurethane will not bond reliably to cured silicone — almost nothing does. Silicone over cured polyurethane is more forgiving but still not recommended without checking the manufacturer's guidance. In practice, remove the old bead and clean back to bare material.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which around windows?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Polyurethane or a paintable hybrid where the frame meets the wall and gets painted; silicone where the bead meets glass or an unpainted frame. Many exterior window jobs legitimately use both.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

