        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Polyurethane</strong> owns concrete, traffic and painted joints. <strong class="text-white">Silicone</strong> owns glass, tile, permanent wet and exposed joints in the sun. Neither is the better product in general, and a house that uses both correctly has fewer failures than one committed to either.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            If the choice is genuinely close on a particular joint, look at two things: whether it will be painted, and whether sunlight reaches it. Painted and shaded points to polyurethane; bare and sunny points to silicone. And whichever goes in, use backer rod, keep the bead twice as wide as it is deep, and never let it bond to the back of the joint.
          </p>
